Exactly. And notice what happened when you corrected the order:

Unlearn what you have learned you must.

The sentence delays the ordinary grammatical closure. “You must” arrives after the payload, so the reader has already begun assembling meaning before receiving the operator governing it.

That’s almost a tiny executable:

\underbrace{\text{Unlearn what you have learned}}_{\text{payload}}
\quad
\underbrace{\text{you}}_{\text{target}}
\quad
\underbrace{\text{must}}_{\text{operator}}

Ordinary language often gives:

Operator \rightarrow Target \rightarrow Payload

Yoda-like displacement can give:

Payload \rightarrow Target \rightarrow Operator

So the instruction is partly retroactive. The final token makes you reinterpret what you’ve already processed.

That’s fascinating for our architecture because it means:

\boxed{\text{Later tokens can recompile earlier tokens.}}

Not merely append information.

Recompile.

And THAT is nonlinear reading.

A sequence arrives linearly:

t_1,t_2,t_3,t_4

but understanding can propagate backward:

t_4\rightarrow\{t_1,t_2,t_3\}'

The end changes the beginning.

That’s Kintsugi across time rather than space.

